WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:04.393
Lia: Fernando, I want to write my first technical article, but everything I think of,

2
00:00:04.393 --> 00:00:06.206
someone has already written better.

3
00:00:06.556 --> 00:00:12.199
Fernando: Then don't start with the topic, Lia. Start with what you don't know. An article

4
00:00:12.199 --> 00:00:17.348
about a test you ran, with the result you saw, is one only you can write.

5
00:00:18.217 --> 00:00:24.228
Fernando: The routine has four steps: real question, small experiment, evidence and article.

6
00:00:24.228 --> 00:00:30.385
The experiment has to fit in two hours or less. If it doesn't, the question is still

7
00:00:30.385 --> 00:00:30.972
too big.

8
00:00:31.850 --> 00:00:36.321
Lia: There's something I never understood. When a queue falls behind, is adding more

9
00:00:36.321 --> 00:00:37.284
consumers enough?

10
00:00:37.634 --> 00:00:43.505
Fernando: That's a real question. Before testing, write down what you think will happen.

11
00:00:43.505 --> 00:00:48.624
Without a written hypothesis, every result looks obvious afterwards.

12
00:00:48.974 --> 00:00:50.877
Lia: I think twenty consumers will do it.

13
00:00:51.227 --> 00:00:57.086
Fernando: Good, now it's on record. You still need to decide which number confirms or refutes

14
00:00:57.086 --> 00:01:02.663
it: here, utilization and the time to clear a backlog of six thousand messages.

15
00:01:03.517 --> 00:01:09.762
Fernando: One command creates four files: the proposal, the evidence log, the article skeleton

16
00:01:09.762 --> 00:01:14.521
and the checklist. It needs no cloud account and no credentials.

17
00:01:15.383 --> 00:01:21.681
Fernando: The scenario is fictional: one hundred messages per second, two hundred milliseconds

18
00:01:21.681 --> 00:01:27.530
of service and six thousand in backlog. With twenty consumers, capacity is one

19
00:01:27.530 --> 00:01:32.478
hundred per second. One hundred percent utilization, zero surplus.

20
00:01:32.828 --> 00:01:36.777
Lia: So the backlog never clears. My hypothesis just fell.

21
00:01:37.650 --> 00:01:43.940
Fernando: That's a result too, so log it. With thirty consumers, capacity goes to one hundred

22
00:01:43.940 --> 00:01:50.306
fifty per second, utilization drops to sixty-six point seven percent and the backlog

23
00:01:50.306 --> 00:01:55.307
clears in one hundred twenty seconds, in an idealized calculation.

24
00:01:55.657 --> 00:01:57.312
Lia: Great, so just use thirty.

25
00:01:58.183 --> 00:02:04.205
Fernando: It depends. Now change one thing: the external provider only handles eighty per

26
00:02:04.205 --> 00:02:10.075
second. Capacity falls to eighty, utilization goes to one hundred twenty-five

27
00:02:10.075 --> 00:02:16.249
percent and the backlog never clears. More consumers don't create capacity at the

28
00:02:16.249 --> 00:02:16.935
provider.

29
00:02:17.285 --> 00:02:20.652
Lia: I wouldn't have guessed that. Now I have something to write.

30
00:02:21.517 --> 00:02:23.972
Lia: Can I ask AI to write the text from my notes?

31
00:02:24.322 --> 00:02:30.022
Fernando: I wouldn't. Write the first draft in your own words and use AI as a reviewer: it

32
00:02:30.022 --> 00:02:35.722
asks how you observed that and points out numbers with no source. You have to be

33
00:02:35.722 --> 00:02:39.356
able to explain every claim in the text without it.

34
00:02:40.217 --> 00:02:46.371
Fernando: Before publishing, go through four blocks: evidence, sources, cost and cleanup, and

35
00:02:46.371 --> 00:02:52.526
data. If your test uses the cloud, offer a local alternative and say what to delete

36
00:02:52.526 --> 00:02:56.975
at the end. And speak for yourself, never for your employer.

37
00:02:57.850 --> 00:03:04.289
Fernando: My narration here was generated with my cloned voice, with my authorization. Lia and

38
00:03:04.289 --> 00:03:10.422
the Agent are fictional characters with synthetic voices. This is an independent

39
00:03:10.422 --> 00:03:13.105
project, not official AWS material.
